// A conversion needing *two* user-defined constructors chained in one
// implicit conversion sequence (`int` -> `Inner` via `Inner(int)`, then
// `Inner` -> `Outer` via `Outer(Inner)`) is rejected, exactly as C++
// rejects two user-defined conversions in one implicit conversion
// sequence -- and it must be rejected identically at every boundary.
//
// This lives here rather than in movecheck_test because movecheck does
// not adjudicate the rule at all: it accepts every one of these four
// shapes and defers to codegen. The movecheck-side version of this
// test passed only because its example used `nullptr`, which was an
// untyped Identifier expression and so matched nothing anywhere -- it
// never exercised conversion chaining. Now that `nullptr` has type
// `nullptr_t`, that example is legitimately accepted (real C++ accepts
// it too), so the real rule needed a real example and a harness that
// runs the phase enforcing it.
import std;

class Inner {
  public:
    int v;
    virtual ~Inner() = default;
    Inner(int value) : v{value} {}
    Inner(const Inner& other) : v{other.v} {}
};

class Outer {
  public:
    int v;
    virtual ~Outer() = default;
    Outer(Inner inner) : v{inner.v} {}
    Outer(double unused, int value) : v{value} {}
};

int main() {
    Outer o = 7;
    return 0;
}
